### fsociety: Mr. Robot, Darlene, crew, plan board.

=== mr_robot
-- start
? q.mq_robot>=50 & !q.mq_robot.done & in.world -> robot_pier
? q.mq_robot>=50 & !q.mq_robot.done -> pier_wait
? flag.joined_fsociety -> member
? q.mq_fsociety>=30 -> arcade_intro
? q.mq_fsociety>=20 -> on_way
> A man in a worn field jacket sits on the last bench of the platform like he owns the whole line. A patch on the jacket, half picked off: MR. ROBOT. He has been watching you since before you saw him.
MR. ROBOT: There he is. You put it on Ron's box. Of course you did. You always finish what you start, kid, even when you can't remember starting it.
! quest mq_rootkit done
ELLIOT: I don't know you.
MR. ROBOT: Sure you do. You just don't know that you do. Sit down. Trains are late. They're always late. It's the only honest thing about this city.
* "You put that file on my computer." -> file
* "What do you want?" -> want
* [SPEECH 35] "You know where I live, what I run, how I name my variables. Start talking, or I start digging." -> dig | want
-- file
MR. ROBOT: I put it where you'd find it. Who wrote it, that's a better question, and you're not ready for the answer. Doesn't matter tonight. What matters is you read it, you understood it, and you used it. You liked it. Don't lie to me. I'd know.
-> want
-- dig
MR. ROBOT: Dig. Ha. You'd find a dead end with your own fingerprints on it. Fine. Short version: there are some people who think the same thing you think at three in the morning. That the world is a rigged game and the house never loses. They've stopped thinking it and started doing something about it.
! trust robot 1
-> want
-- want
MR. ROBOT: I want you to come and meet some friends. Coney Island. There's an arcade on the boardwalk, half the machines don't work, the other half are rigged. Fun Society. Come tonight, tomorrow, whenever. They'll be there. So will I.
ELLIOT (V.O.): A train comes in. Doors open, close. When I look back at the bench, he's standing at the stairs, already leaving, like he timed it.
* "Who are you?" -> who
* "Why should I?" -> why
-- who
MR. ROBOT: The guy who's been waiting for you a long time. Longer than you'd believe. Come to Coney, kid. You'll want to see this. You've wanted it your whole life.
! quest mq_fsociety 20
-> END
-- why
MR. ROBOT: Because you already do this. Every night. You read people, you judge them, you decide who's guilty, and then you tip off a cop and go to bed and nothing changes. I'm offering you the thing that changes.
! quest mq_fsociety 20
-> END
-- pier_wait
MR. ROBOT: Not here, kid. Not with them listening. Tonight. The end of the pier. You know the one.
-> END
-- on_way
MR. ROBOT: Coney. Fun Society. Don't make me come get you.
-> END
-- arcade_intro
? flag.heard_plan -> ask_again
> The back room of the arcade. Monitors, cables, a whiteboard rubbed almost clean. Four people stop talking when you walk in.
MR. ROBOT: Everybody, Elliot. Elliot, everybody. Darlene writes the code that makes grown engineers cry. Mobley builds things out of other things. Trenton keeps us honest, which is the hardest job here. And you know me. More or less.
DARLENE: Hey. Long time. ...Don't look at me like that. Later.
MR. ROBOT: Now. Here's what we're going to do to the biggest company on Earth.
! set heard_plan
> E Corp holds seventy percent of the world's consumer debt: student loans, mortgages, credit cards, medical bills. Every one of those numbers lives in their data centers.
MR. ROBOT: We don't delete it. Deleting gets restored by lunch. We encrypt it. All of it, in one night, with a key nobody keeps. The records still exist; they just become noise. No one owes anything to anyone, because nobody can read the ledger. The biggest redistribution of wealth in history, and it happens in about four minutes.
TRENTON: Or it crashes the economy and people who already have nothing lose the little they have. We argue about that every night. You should hear both sides before you pick one.
MOBLEY: There's a catch. Several. E Corp keeps tape backups in a vault upstate called Steel Mountain. We need someone inside their network. And we need more muscle than five people with a soldering iron. Every piece of this has someone else's hands on it.
MR. ROBOT: Which means you're going to meet some people. Some of them are worse than E Corp. You'll have to decide which ones you can stomach. So. Are you in?
-> decide
-- ask_again
MR. ROBOT: You heard the plan. The door's still open. Are you in?
-> decide
-- decide
* "I'm in." -> yes ! set joined_fsociety ; quest mq_fsociety 40 ; trust robot 2 ; fame fsociety 6
* [if !flag.thought_once] "I need to think." -> think
* "Trenton's right to worry. Tell me how you keep it from hurting the people at the bottom." -> trenton_q
* [require trust.robot<0] "No. I don't trust you. I don't think you're even real." -> refuse
-- trenton_q
TRENTON: Thank you. Someone asked. ...Food banks, pharmacies, the small stuff, it runs on cash and credit unions; we leave those alone. The hard part is the week after, when nobody knows what anything is worth. We plan for that week. Or we don't do this.
MR. ROBOT: See? Conscience. Every good crew needs one. Now. Yes or no?
! set asked_week_after ; trust darlene 1
-> decide
-- yes
MR. ROBOT: That's my kid. Welcome home. Three threads. The mountain: Mobley's device cooks the tapes. The army: someone with real reach, the Dark Army in Chinatown, or the Feds looking the wrong way, and you will meet both whether you like it or not. And the door: a way inside E Corp itself. It's all on the board.
MR. ROBOT: But one thing at a time. The mountain first. Without it, nothing else matters. Mobley's device is in the stash.
! quest mq_steel 10
MR. ROBOT: The other threads will find you, or you can go find them; they're up on the board whenever you want to read them. Every choice you make out there changes what this night looks like. When it's all in place, you come back here and we press the key. Together.
-> END
-- think
MR. ROBOT: Think. Sure. You'll think, and you'll come back, because thinking is just the noise you make on your way to the thing you were always going to do. Door's open.
! set thought_once
-> END
-- refuse
MR. ROBOT: Not real. That's cute. Ask yourself who's been finishing your sentences your whole life, kid. Then come find me when you're done pretending.
! quest mq_fsociety 40 ; set refused_fsociety
-> END
-- member
? flag.five_nine_done -> after_hack
MR. ROBOT: Status report. We need Steel Mountain cooked, an ally faction on side, and E Corp's door open. Then we end the world's debt in one night. How close are we?
* "Show me where we stand. Are we ready?" -> finale_check
* [if stab<40] "Are you even real?" -> whisper
* "Tell me again why we're doing this." -> why2
-- after_hack
MR. ROBOT: It's done, kid. Five/Nine happened. Now go sit at their table at the Salina and finish it. That's the last door. I'll be right here. I'm always right here.
-> END
-- close
MR. ROBOT: Good. Keep moving. The window doesn't stay open forever, and neither do people.
-> END
-- why2
MR. ROBOT: Because you tried it their way. You took the meds, you sat in Krista's chair, you guarded their networks like a good dog. And you still wanted to scream. This is the scream, kid. Let it out.
! trust robot 1
-> END
-- whisper
MR. ROBOT: You're fraying. I can see it. Take the meds if you have to — I'll get quieter, but I won't leave. I never leave. That's the deal we made. You just don't remember making it.
! glitch 0.8 ; stab -2
-> END

=== darlene
-- start
? q.mq_robot>=10 & q.mq_robot<30 & in.darlene_apt -> darlene_home
? flag.five_nine_done & q.mq_after>=20 & !q.mq_after.done -> morning_after
? flag.five_nine_done & !q.mq_after>=20 & in.arcade -> morning_after_early
? companion.darlene_n -> comp_darlene_n
? flag.joined_fsociety -> crew
? q.mq_fsociety>=10 -> street
DARLENE: You don't remember me, do you. That's fine. That's on-brand. Just — walk with me. Don't make it weird.
-> END
-- morning_after_early
DARLENE: Go home first. Sleep. Watch the news. Then come back and tell me if it still feels like winning.
-> END
-- street
DARLENE: Okay. So. You met him. The jacket guy. He talks like a prophet and smells like a subway, and he's usually right, which is the annoying part.
ELLIOT (V.O.): There's something about her. A familiarity that itches. Like a file I deleted but didn't overwrite.
* "Who are you to me?" -> who
* "Is he for real?" -> real
* [SPEECH 40] "You look at me like you know me. Stop it." -> push | who
-- who
DARLENE: Wow. Okay. We'll get to that. When you're ready. Which is never, historically. Come to the arcade. Bring the mask energy.
! trust darlene 1
-> END
-- real
DARLENE: Real enough to blow up the world's biggest company. Real enough that I've been running his code for months. Whether he's REAL real — that's a you question. You've always had a complicated relationship with real.
-> END
-- push
DARLENE: ...God, you're exhausting when you're like this. Fine. I'll stop. For now. But you're going to want the answer someday, and I'll be right here, being annoyed that I gave it to you.
! trust darlene 1 ; set darlene_pushed
-> END
-- crew
? trust.darlene>=6 -> bond
DARLENE: There he is. My favorite disaster. We're this close, Elliot. This close to the biggest thing anyone's ever done. And you're going to try to do it alone, aren't you. Because that's your whole personality.
* "Not alone. You're here." -> together ! trust darlene 2
* [if !flag.left_darlene_n & !flag.five_nine_done] "Come with me. Out there. I could use you." -> comp_darlene_n.recruit
* "Alone is safer. For you." -> alone
* [SPEECH 45] "Tell me the truth about us. All of it." -> truth | alone
-- together
DARLENE: Yeah. I'm here. I'm always here, even when you forget I exist. Especially then. Don't get us killed, okay? I'd like to survive knowing you.
! set darlene_close
-> END
-- alone
DARLENE: Safer. Right. You keep everyone at arm's length and call it protection. It's not. It's just lonely with a better excuse.
-> END
-- truth
DARLENE: We're family, Elliot. That's the truth you keep deleting. I'm your sister. You had a whole other version of tonight where you knew that the second you saw me. Come back to that version. I miss it.
! trust darlene 3 ; set knows_darlene_sister
-> END
-- bond
? flag.darlene_bond -> bond_again
DARLENE: Hey. I know things are loud in there right now. Whatever happens with the hack — you're my brother. That's the one file that never corrupts. Come here, you idiot.
! set darlene_bond ; heal
-> bond_again
-- bond_again
DARLENE: What do you need, brother?
* [if !flag.left_darlene_n & !flag.five_nine_done] "Come with me. Out there." -> comp_darlene_n.recruit
* "Nothing. Just wanted to see you." -> END

=== mobley
-- start
MOBLEY: You're the new guy. Or the old guy, depending who you ask around here. I built the thing for Steel Mountain — a Raspberry Pi that talks to the climate controllers and turns the whole vault into an oven. Elegant, right?
* "Where's the device?" -> device
* "Will it work?" -> work
* [if !item.steel_badge] [HACKING 35] "Can you forge me a Steel Mountain visitor badge?" -> badge | nobadge
-- device
MOBLEY: In the stash, when you're ready to carry it in. Get past their security, plug it into the climate unit, and the tapes cook themselves. Don't get caught holding it. That's a felony you can't explain.
! set has_pi_plan
-> END
-- badge
MOBLEY: If you can pull their visitor schema off the public contractor portal — yeah, you just did, show-off — I can print it. Laminated. Barcode. A name that isn't yours. Don't smile in the photo; nobody who works there smiles.
! give steel_badge 1 ; set steel_badge_used
-> END
-- nobadge
MOBLEY: I'd need their badge schema and you'd need to not fumble the portal login. You fumbled. Buy the coveralls at Ricky's. Nobody looks at the maintenance guy.
-> END
-- work
MOBLEY: It'll work. I don't build things that don't work. People, sure, those I'm bad at. Machines never disappoint me. That's why I like them.
-> END

=== trenton
-- start
? q.sq_cloud>=40 & !q.sq_cloud.done -> trenton_cloud
? companion.trenton_n -> comp_trenton_n
? flag.trenton_talked -> again
! set trenton_talked
TRENTON: I keep telling everyone — if we do this, we don't get to take it back. People think of debt as a chain. But for some of them it's the only record that they exist at all. Erase it and you erase them too. I still think we should do it. I just want us to know what we're doing.
* "We're freeing people." -> free
* "Nothing's ever clean. We do it anyway." -> anyway ! trust darlene 0
-- free
TRENTON: Most of them. Yeah. Just... not all. Keep that number in your head when it happens. Somebody should.
-> END
-- anyway
TRENTON: That's honest, at least. I'll take honest over a hero any day. Heroes get people killed and feel great about it.
-> END
-- again
TRENTON: Elliot. Still keeping the number in your head?
* [if flag.joined_fsociety & !flag.left_trenton_n & !flag.five_nine_done] "Come with me. I want someone careful out there." -> comp_trenton_n.recruit
* "Always." -> END

=== arcade_planboard
-- start
ELLIOT (V.O.): The wall is covered in string and printouts. The whole plan, laid out like a nervous system. Steel Mountain. The Dark Army. E Corp's tower. Three threads, all leading to one night.
> To finish this, you need three things: Steel Mountain's backups destroyed, one major faction backing the play, and a door open inside E Corp itself.
* Study the Steel Mountain thread. -> steel
* Study the faction thread. -> faction
* Study the E Corp thread. -> ecorp
* Step back. -> END
-- steel
ELLIOT (V.O.): Steel Mountain. Upstate-north. Mobley's device does the work — I just have to walk it past the guards. A badge, or a uniform, gets me in the door.
! quest mq_steel 10
-> END
-- faction
ELLIOT (V.O.): We can't do this alone. The Dark Army — Whiterose's people — could. Or the FBI could be fed just enough to look the wrong way. Two doors. Very different rooms.
! quest mq_darkarmy 10
-> END
-- ecorp
ELLIOT (V.O.): The tower. Tyrell Wellick wants to be CTO badly enough to hand us the keys. Or I take a keycard off a guard and let myself in. Either way, the last door is inside.
! quest mq_ecorp 10
-> END

### The Cloud: Raj and the Basement.

=== raj
-- start
? flag.raj_badge -> after
> A man in an E Corp Cloud lanyard at the bar, staring into a beer like it's a status dashboard. His badge is clipped to his jacket. He keeps touching it, like it might bite.
RAJ: If you're from work, I'm off the clock. If you're not from work, I'm still off the clock. I've been off the clock for eleven years, spiritually.
* "You work at the data center." -> work
* "Rough night?" -> work
-- work
RAJ: I run the shredder that doesn't shred. Retention operations. People hit delete, and my job is to make sure nothing gets deleted. Four hundred petabytes of other people's regrets. My mother's in there. Her deleted voicemails to me. I found them. I listen to them sometimes. That's how you know it's a bad job.
* [SPEECH 45] "Then help me empty it. One night. Nobody will ever know it was you." -> yes | no
* [if cash>=300] "Three hundred for the badge. You lost it. People lose badges." -> buy
* "That's rough. Good night, Raj." -> END
-- yes
> He's quiet for a long time. Then he unclips the badge and puts it on the bar between you, face down, like a card he's folding.
RAJ: Staff door's on the south side. The cameras run through a junction box on the avenue, west side; the vendor never changed the password, I've reported it four times. Don't go in with the cameras on. And if you see the folder called MEHTA_S... leave it. Or don't. I don't know anymore.
! give dc_badge 1 ; set raj_badge ; quest sq_cloud 20 ; xp 80
-> END
-- no
RAJ: (He laughs, not nicely.) Help you. A stranger in a hoodie. I've worked for E Corp too long to believe in strangers. Buy me a beer sometime. Maybe I'll believe in you.
-> END
-- buy
RAJ: ...You know what? Sure. I lost it. It fell out of my pocket into your hand. Staff door's on the south side, and loop the cameras first, junction box on the avenue. I'm reporting it lost in the morning. Make it count before then.
! cash -300 ; give dc_badge 1 ; set raj_badge ; quest sq_cloud 20 ; xp 50
-> END
-- after
RAJ: I told them I lost it. They docked me forty dollars for the replacement. Best forty dollars I ever spent.
-> END

=== trenton_cloud
-- start
? flag.cloud_sold -> sold
? flag.cloud_told -> told
TRENTON: You wiped it. All of it. Four hundred petabytes of things people wanted gone, and now they're gone, like they asked. Nobody will ever know. That's the strange part. The best thing we've done, and the whole point is nobody finds out.
! quest sq_cloud done ; fame fsociety 3 ; trust trenton 2 ; xp 100
-> END
-- told
TRENTON: My phone's been buzzing for three hours. Everyone I know got the email. My mother got the email. She called me crying because they kept the photos of my father she deleted after the funeral. Then she said 'good, now I have them back.' I don't know what to feel. I think that's correct.
! quest sq_cloud done ; fame fsociety 4 ; trust trenton 3 ; xp 120
-> END
-- sold
TRENTON: Five thousand dollars. (She looks at you for a long time.) I'm not going to say anything. I want you to notice that I'm not saying anything.
! quest sq_cloud done ; trust trenton -2 ; xp 60
-> END
